1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:01,976
This is the Rare Disease Atlas.

2
00:00:02,026 --> 00:00:04,397
Start from any disease, gene or symptom.

3
00:00:04,447 --> 00:00:05,850
Here I search a disease.

4
00:00:06,150 --> 00:00:11,850
It resolves the name to Krabbe disease, with its genes, mechanism, rare
symptoms, prevalence and patient groups.

5
00:00:12,150 --> 00:00:15,692
A different disease family brings up adrenoleukodystrophy.

6
00:00:15,742 --> 00:00:18,850
The evidence panel shows the source and confidence.

7
00:00:19,150 --> 00:00:23,967
The map shows its neighbourhood, with line styles for records, papers
and computed links.

8
00:00:24,017 --> 00:00:27,850
Tabs list NIH funding, researchers, and existing trials and registries.

9
00:00:28,150 --> 00:00:32,414
The action plan rates this pair a supported lead with moderate
readiness.

10
00:00:32,464 --> 00:00:37,850
It lists caveats like stopped trials and no shared gene, plus studies
and people to contact.

11
00:00:38,150 --> 00:00:41,850
Explain simply gives the result in plain language, with every sentence
cited.

12
00:00:42,150 --> 00:00:44,999
Ask the literature answers from PubMed abstracts.

13
00:00:45,049 --> 00:00:49,850
Each source is listed so it can be checked, and unsupported questions
are refused.

14
00:00:50,150 --> 00:00:57,850
A second example: these two diseases share a bile acid pathway, so
readiness is strong, with a registry and study to reuse.
